Critical launch blocker: subscription access can likely be self-enabled

This is the most serious issue I found.

The profiles table contains:

subscription_active
subscribed_at

The migration gives authenticated users update access to their own profile rows. The RLS policy checks only that the row belongs to the current user; it does not prevent the user from updating subscription columns. The same migration also grants every authenticated user permission to execute the demo activate_search_subscription() function, which directly sets subscription_active = true.

Later migrations correctly restrict the Stripe webhook RPC to the service role, but they do not appear to revoke the original profile update privilege or the demo activation RPC.

From the SQL as written, a knowledgeable authenticated user could likely bypass payment by either:

Updating their own subscription_active field, or
Calling activate_search_subscription() directly

Treat this as a P0 launch blocker.

Required correction
Remove direct authenticated update permission from billing fields.
Remove or revoke the demo activation RPC in production.
Allow only the Stripe webhook/service role to change subscription state.
Put editable user fields such as display_name behind a narrowly scoped RPC or separate profile table.
Consider a separate subscriptions table that authenticated users can read but never update.


